==============================================================================
WARNINGS ON THE INVENTORY ITSELF
==============================================================================

  1. A BINARY JAR IS DOWNLOADED AT BUILD TIME WITH NO INTEGRITY CHECK
     The Dockerfile pulls gson-2.11.0.jar over the network with
       ADD https://search.maven.org/remotecontent?filepath=...
     and verifies neither a checksum nor a signature. Whatever that URL
     returns is redistributed inside the published image and linked into
     /usr/java/lib/launcher.jar. The inventory in LICENSE and NOTICE assumes
     the URL serves the Apache-2.0 licensed Google Gson 2.11.0 artifact; that
     assumption is not enforced by the build. Pin the artifact by SHA-256, or
     vendor it, so that what is licensed here is what is shipped.

  2. THE BASE IMAGE IS OUT OF STANDARD SUPPORT
     ibm-semeru-runtimes:open-17.0.12.1_7-jdk-focal is built on Ubuntu 20.04
     (focal), whose standard support ended in April 2025, and pins Java
     17.0.12.1. Newer operating-system packages than those recorded here may
     be required, and each rebuild of a fixed base tag can still change the
     bundled package set through `apt-get upgrade`.

  3. WHICH PYTHON IS INSTALLED IS NOT PINNED
     The Dockerfile installs the `python` package and bin/compile runs under
     `#!/usr/bin/env python`. On Ubuntu 20.04 that name is the Python 2
     interpreter, which reached end of life on 2020-01-01; Python 3 is a
     different package. The Python Software Foundation License text
     reproduced in LICENSE is the one that ships with CPython 2.7. Confirm
     what the build actually installs and pin it.

  4. TRANSITIVE AND INHERITED PACKAGES ARE NOT ENUMERATED
     `apt-get install locales python` pulls further Ubuntu packages, and the
     base image ships an entire JDK and userland. Their copyright files are in
     /usr/share/doc/<package>/copyright inside the image. They are part of the
     convenience binary, not of the source release; a complete per-package
     inventory can only be produced from a built image.

  5. LICENSE TEXTS WERE TAKEN FROM UPSTREAM, NOT FROM A BUILT IMAGE
     The texts concatenated into LICENSE come from the upstream projects: the
     OpenJDK and Eclipse OpenJ9 LICENSE files, the GNU GPL 2.0 and LGPL 2.1
     as published by the FSF, the CPython 2.7 LICENSE, and for the Go
     dependencies the module versions recorded in go.mod. The distributions
     may ship different revisions of the same texts, and the authoritative
     copy for the shipped binaries remains /usr/share/doc/<package>/copyright
     and the JDK's own legal directory inside the image.

  6. THE BUNDLED PROXY CANNOT BE VERIFIED FROM THIS DIRECTORY
     /bin/proxy is copied from the image named by the COMMON build argument,
     which defaults to `missing:missing`. The Go module inventory in LICENSE
     and NOTICE was derived from the go.mod and go.sum of this repository. If
     the image passed as COMMON is built from a different source tree, the
     modules actually linked into /bin/proxy may differ.

  7. DEPENDENCIES OF USER ACTIONS ARE NOT COVERED
     Beyond Gson, this image ships no preinstalled Java libraries: user
     actions bring their own jars. Those libraries are NOT part of this image
     and NOT part of any Apache release artifact, and no claim is made here
     about their licenses.

  8. THE SAME INVENTORY APPLIES TO THE OTHER JAVA RUNTIMES
     This Dockerfile differs from runtime/java/v8, v11 and v21 only in the
     ibm-semeru-runtimes base tag and in the VERSION, UPDATE and BUILD
     environment variables, and all four download the same Gson 2.11.0 jar from
     the same unverified URL. bin/compile is identical to the one in
     runtime/java/v11. lib/src/Launcher.java differs from the v8 and v11 copy
     only in indentation, in using java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets, and in
     the security manager change described in warning 9; none of that changes
     the bundled-software inventory, which is therefore identical across the
     four Java images apart from the JDK version and must be updated in all of
     them together. This image is not the default one: runtime/java/v8 is.

  9. THE ACTION SANDBOX IS DISABLED IN THIS IMAGE
     lib/src/Launcher.java has its installSecurityManager() and
     uninstallSecurityManager() methods commented out, with the note that
     SecurityManager is deprecated from JDK 17 and that no alternative is yet
     available. The v8 and v11 images install a security manager that turns an
     action's System.exit() into a SecurityException; this image does not, so
     an action can terminate the runtime process. This is a behavioural
     difference between the Java images, not a licensing one, and it is
     recorded here because it is not visible from the Dockerfile.


